<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
    </style>


    <!-- The launch screen.

         windowSplashScreenBackground is the one line here that decides what
         the player sees when they tap the icon. Without it the app opened
         WHITE and then jumped to the dark teal of the game. The chain, read
         out of the merged resources of a real build: androidx values-v31 maps
         the platform attribute android:windowSplashScreenBackground onto this
         compat one, and Theme.SplashScreen leaves the compat one at
         ?android:colorBackground, whose theme resolves through
         Base.Theme.SplashScreen.DayNight to Base.Theme.SplashScreen.Light --
         white, in the light mode nearly everyone is in. There is no
         values-night here, so dark mode does not save it either.

         The android:background line Capacitor template writes here is gone,
         and so are the twenty-six drawable/splash images it pointed at. They
         were never drawn on any version -- a window takes its background from
         android:windowBackground, which androidx Theme.SplashScreen.Common
         sets to its own compat drawable, and on API 31 and up the platform
         draws the splash from the attribute above. Nothing else referenced
         them and the layout has no background attribute either. They cost
         5.51 MB on disk and 5083 KB of a 10.78 MB debug APK: forty-seven per
         cent of the app was a picture nobody could ever see. Half of them
         were landscape variants, in an app locked to portrait.

         npm run assets would put both back. check-android.js refuses the
         build if they return, and says why.

         postSplashScreenTheme is deliberately NOT set. It is an androidx
         attribute, honoured only by installSplashScreen(), and this app uses
         a plain BridgeActivity that never calls it -- so it would be a line
         that reads as configuration and does nothing. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
    </style>
</resources>